THE STARS DON'T WAIT
Carve out a life in a galaxy already in motion.

Version @PROJECT_VERSION@
Platform: @SOL_PACKAGE_PLATFORM@


RUNNING
-------
@SOL_PACKAGE_RUN@

This is a portable build. Unpack it anywhere you like and run it from there;
there is nothing to install and nothing to uninstall. Deleting this folder
removes the game.


REQUIREMENTS
------------
@SOL_PACKAGE_REQUIREMENTS@


WHERE YOUR SAVE AND SETTINGS LIVE
---------------------------------
Not in this folder. The game keeps its save and its settings in a per-user
location, so the folder you unpacked stays read-only as far as the game is
concerned - which means it still works if you unpack it somewhere you cannot
write, and it survives replacing this folder with a newer version.

  Windows:  %LOCALAPPDATA%\The Stars Don't Wait\
  Linux:    $XDG_DATA_HOME/the-stars-dont-wait/
            (or ~/.local/share/the-stars-dont-wait/ if that is unset)

To reset the game completely, delete that directory.


MODS
----
The mods/ folder beside this file is empty and that is not a mistake. Each
first-level subdirectory of mods/ is one mod, laid over the base game's data
in name order - so mods/zebra/ships.toml wins over mods/alpha/ships.toml, and
both win over data/ships.toml. Ids replace wholesale rather than merging.

Installing a mod is copying its folder into mods/. Nothing else; there is no
manager and no load order to edit.

To MAKE one, there is a second download: THE SOL FORGE, the authoring tool
this game is built with. It opens a mod folder and builds meshes, textures,
sounds, materials and the def rows that tie them together, then cooks them
into the form this game loads. It is a separate archive on purpose - a player
who will never mod should not be handed an editor - so this folder contains no
editor and never will.


COMMAND LINE
------------
  --seed <number>   Generate a specific galaxy. The same seed always produces
                    the same galaxy.
  --hardcore        Death deletes the save.
  --frames <count>  Run for a fixed number of frames and exit. Used by the
                    project's own smoke tests.


